  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Airhub:</strong> ESIMOFFER5, {{ airhubBest.discount | lower }}. Airhub's own code, {{ providerA.primaryCode }}, isn't currently applying at checkout. <a href="/airhub/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Holafly:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ holaflyPrimary.discount | lower }}. This one's ours. <a href="/holafly/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Holafly's code is ours, so we show it prominently. Airhub's best working code right now is a public one, not ours, since our own Airhub code has a reported issue at checkout.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Airhub or Holafly the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Airhub if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You can estimate roughly how many GB you'll use</li>
        <li>You want SMS and a local number bundled with your data, not sold as an extra</li>
        <li>You're headed somewhere Airhub prices sharply, like Thailand or Japan</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Holafly if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You genuinely don't know how much data you'll use</li>
        <li>Heavy streaming, video calls, or hotspot use</li>
        <li>China is on the itinerary and you want the built-in VPN</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Airhub bundles SMS and a local number; Holafly doesn't sell either.</strong> If you need to receive a verification text or hand out a number that isn't your own while traveling, Airhub covers that without an add-on. Neither provider includes voice calls, though: if you need a real number with calls guaranteed, <a href="/simlocal/">SimLocal</a>'s UK plans are a better fit than either of these two.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Holafly's China eSIM has a VPN built in.</strong> Google Maps, WhatsApp, and Gmail work over the Holafly connection without installing a separate VPN app first. Airhub has no equivalent, so if China is on the trip, you'd need your own VPN solution regardless of which Airhub plan you buy.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Airhub vs Holafly FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Airhub or Holafly</h3>
      <p>Airhub, if you can estimate your data use ahead of time: its per-GB bundle pricing is sharply lower than Holafly's flat daily rate in most destinations. Holafly can work out cheaper (or at least less stressful) if you'd otherwise buy multiple Airhub bundles to cover heavy same-day use.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either one include a phone number</h3>
      <p>Airhub includes a local number in many destinations alongside the data. Holafly is data-only and doesn't offer a number. Neither includes voice calls.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for China</h3>
      <p>Holafly, since its China eSIM has a VPN built in so common apps and sites work without extra setup. Airhub has no equivalent, so a separate VPN would be needed regardless of plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I use one for a multi-country trip and the other for a single-country trip</h3>
      <p>Yes. A common approach is Holafly's unlimited regional plan for a data-heavy multi-country leg, and Airhub's cheaper per-GB bundles for a light, predictable single-country stop.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

